From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 23:37:03 +0200
Subject: riscv: dts: sophgo: mux the Milk-V Duo S eMMC pads from the DT

On a Duo S with eMMC fitted, booting from a card leaves the eMMC invisible:
the controller registers and then

  mmc2: SDHCI controller on 4300000.mmc [4300000.mmc] using ADMA 64-bit
  mmc2: Failed to initialize a non-removable card

The chip is fine and it is not a bus conflict with the card slot - the two are
separate controllers and the installer drives both in one U-Boot session. The
eMMC's seven pads are simply never routed. In the vendor bootloader,
board/cvitek/cv181x/board.c does

  #if defined(CONFIG_NAND_SUPPORT)
          pinmux_config(PINMUX_SPI_NAND);
  #elif defined(CONFIG_SPI_FLASH)
          pinmux_config(PINMUX_SPI_NOR);
  #elif defined(CONFIG_EMMC_SUPPORT)
          pinmux_config(PINMUX_EMMC);
  #endif

and CONFIG_EMMC_SUPPORT comes from STORAGE_TYPE=emmc at bootloader build time.
So the eMMC build routes them and the SD build does not - the same seven pads
carry SPI-NAND and SPI-NOR, which is why they are switched rather than fixed.
Linux then inherits whatever the BootROM left, and our device tree said nothing
about them, so nothing corrected it.

Declaring them here makes it independent of which bootloader ran, exactly as the
card slot already is: those pads happen to sit in their native function after
either boot, which is why the slot works both ways today.

Mux and power-source, nothing else. PINMUX_CONFIG() in the vendor bootloader
sets the function select alone and the eMMC runs at those defaults when it is
the boot device, but power-source is not optional:
cv1800_dt_node_to_map_post() reads it with of_property_read_u32() and returns
the error if it is absent, which fails the whole pin map - and then the
consumer does not probe at all, so 4300000.mmc disappears from dmesg entirely
rather than failing visibly.

no-1-8-v is deliberately left alone. Dropping it and declaring mmc-hs200-1_8v
with mmc-ddr-1_8v was tried, on the theory that the vendor bootloader tunes
this controller and so must be running HS200; the eMMC then does not enumerate
at all - the controller registers and no card line ever follows, which is what
mmc_select_hs200() does when the voltage switch fails, since it returns an
error other than -EBADMSG and mmc_select_timing() propagates it instead of
falling back. High speed on a 4-bit bus stands until somebody establishes what
VDDIO_EMMC actually is on this board. mmc-ddr-1_8v on its own is untried.

One consequence worth stating: this device tree is shared with the Duo S that
has no eMMC fitted, where it will now pin seven pads for a chip that is not
there. That is inert unless those pads are wired to something else on that
variant, which nothing in the vendor sources suggests - they carry SPI-NAND or
SPI-NOR on boards that have such flash, and the Duo S has neither.

Tested on a Duo S with eMMC, booted from a card: mmcblk2 appears with its
partitions, plus the boot0/boot1/rpmb areas.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts | 45 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -113,13 +113,56 @@ &dmac {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Route the eMMC pads ourselves instead of inheriting them. The vendor U-Boot
+ * only muxes this group when it was built for eMMC storage
+ * (board/cvitek/cv181x/board.c, guarded by CONFIG_EMMC_SUPPORT), and the same
+ * seven pads are shared with SPI-NAND and SPI-NOR. So after booting the SD build
+ * of the bootloader they are left in whatever the BootROM set, and Linux cannot
+ * talk to the eMMC: "mmc2: Failed to initialize a non-removable card".
+ *
+ * Mux and power-source only. PINMUX_CONFIG() in the vendor bootloader sets the
+ * function select and nothing else, and the eMMC works with those defaults when
+ * it is the boot device - but power-source is not optional here:
+ * cv1800_dt_node_to_map_post() rejects any group without it, which fails the
+ * consumer's probe outright.
+ *
+ * no-1-8-v stays. Dropping it and asking for mmc-hs200-1_8v was tried on a Duo
+ * S with the eMMC fitted, and the card then never enumerates at all: the
+ * controller registers and no card line ever follows, which is the non-graceful
+ * failure mmc_select_hs200() has when the voltage switch does not take. High
+ * speed on a 4-bit bus it is, until somebody establishes what VDDIO_EMMC
+ * actually is on this board. mmc-ddr-1_8v on its own is untried.
+ *
+ * max-frequency matches the vendor DTS. It is inert while no-1-8-v holds the
+ * card at high speed - the clock is set from ext_csd.hs_max_dtr either way -
+ * and is here because it was in the tree that was booted and verified.
+ */
+&pinctrl {
+	emmc_cfg: emmc-cfg {
+		emmc-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_CLK, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_CMD, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_DAT0, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_DAT1, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_DAT2, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_DAT3, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_EMMC_RSTN, 0)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
 &emmc {
 	bus-width = <4>;
 	no-1-8-v;
+	max-frequency = <200000000>;
 	cap-mmc-hw-reset;
 	no-sd;
 	no-sdio;
 	non-removable;
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&emmc_cfg>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
@@ -307,4 +350,4 @@ &int_adc {
 
 &remoteproc {
 	status = "okay";
-};
\ No newline at end of file
+};
-- 
